# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'Ezra'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'history'
SUBTITLE = 'Return from Babylon, the House Rebuilt, and the Law Taught'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Ezra opens with the decree of Cyrus and follows the first return of the people to Jerusalem under Zerubbabel and Jeshua. It records the altar and foundation of the temple, the opposition, the dedication of the house, and the later coming of Ezra the scribe, his prayer of confession, and the separation of the people from the strange wives (Ezra 1:1–3; 3:2, 10–11; 6:16; 7:6, 10; 9:6, 15; 10:11).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'This book belongs entirely to the Prophecy Program, where a remnant of Israel returns to the land to rebuild the house of the LORD under the law of Moses (Ezra 1:3; 7:6).')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Ezra', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — The Return Under Cyrus (Chs. 1–2)'), ('p', 'The LORD stirs up Cyrus, who says, “Who is there among you of all his people? his God be with him, and let him go up to Jerusalem” (Ezra 1:1–3).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — The Altar, the Foundation, and the Opposition (Chs. 3–6)'), ('p', 'The altar is built as written in the law of Moses, the foundation is laid with praise, and the house is dedicated with joy (Ezra 3:2, 10–11; 4:3; 6:16, 22).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — Ezra Goes Up (Chs. 7–8)'), ('p', 'Ezra is a ready scribe in the law of Moses, prepares his heart to seek the law of the LORD, and trusts the hand of God for the way (Ezra 7:6, 10, 27; 8:22).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — Confession and Separation (Chs. 9–10)'), ('p', 'Ezra prays, “O LORD God of Israel, thou art righteous,” the people weep, and they are told to separate from the people of the land (Ezra 9:6, 15; 10:1, 11).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Ezra', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>The LORD Stirs Cyrus</b> — “The LORD stirred up the spirit of Cyrus king of Persia” (Ezra 1:1).', '<b>The House of the LORD God of Israel</b> — “Build the house of the LORD God of Israel, (he is the God,) which is in Jerusalem” (Ezra 1:3).', '<b>As Written in the Law</b> — “As it is written in the law of Moses the man of God” (Ezra 3:2).', '<b>A Heart Prepared</b> — “Ezra had prepared his heart to seek the law of the LORD, and to do it, and to teach in Israel statutes and judgments” (Ezra 7:10).', '<b>Righteous Before Him</b> — “O LORD God of Israel, thou art righteous” (Ezra 9:15).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Ezra', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how Ezra points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Return of a Remnant</b> — The book records a return to the land under Cyrus’s decree (Ezra 1:1–3), reported as written within Israel’s program.', '<b>The House Rebuilt</b> — The house of the LORD is rebuilt and dedicated (Ezra 3:10–11; 6:16).', '<b>The Scribe and the Law</b> — Ezra teaches the law of Moses in Israel (Ezra 7:6, 10).'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in Ezra', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Ezra speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. Ezra operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Ezra in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', 'Ezra reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['the return to the land after exile (Ezra 1:3)', 'altar and house of the LORD (Ezra 3:2; 6:16)', 'the law of Moses (Ezra 7:6, 10)', 'separation from the people of the land (Ezra 10:11)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Ezra that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Ezra contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['The Decree to Go Up — “his God be with him, and let him go up to Jerusalem, which is in Judah.” (Ezra 1:3).', 'The Altar Built — “builded the altar of the God of Israel, to offer burnt offerings thereon.” (Ezra 3:2).', 'The Foundation Laid — “the foundation of the house of the LORD was laid.” (Ezra 3:11).', 'The House Dedicated — “kept the dedication of this house of God with joy.” (Ezra 6:16).', 'A Ready Scribe — “he was a ready scribe in the law of Moses.” (Ezra 7:6).', 'Teaching Israel — “to teach in Israel statutes and judgments.” (Ezra 7:10).', 'Separation — “separate yourselves from the people of the land, and from the strange wives.” (Ezra 10:11).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Ezra Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Ezra and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Ezra teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Ezra', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['The Decree to Go Up', '“his God be with him, and let him go up to Jerusalem, which is in Judah.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Ezra 1:3'], ['The Altar Built', '“builded the altar of the God of Israel, to offer burnt offerings thereon.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Ezra 3:2'], ['The Foundation Laid', '“the foundation of the house of the LORD was laid.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Ezra 3:11'], ['The House Dedicated', '“kept the dedication of this house of God with joy.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Ezra 6:16'], ['A Ready Scribe', '“he was a ready scribe in the law of Moses.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Ezra 7:6'], ['Teaching Israel', '“to teach in Israel statutes and judgments.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Ezra 7:10'], ['Separation', '“separate yourselves from the people of the land, and from the strange wives.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Ezra 10:11']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Ezra that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what Ezra teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['The LORD Moves Kings', '“Blessed be the LORD God of our fathers, which hath put such a thing as this in the king’s heart.”', 'Ezra 7:27'], ['His Hand on Those Who Seek', '“The hand of our God is upon all them for good that seek him.”', 'Ezra 8:22'], ['The LORD Is Righteous', '“O LORD God of Israel, thou art righteous.”', 'Ezra 9:15'], ['His Mercy Endures', '“for his mercy endureth for ever toward Israel.”', 'Ezra 3:11']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Ezra without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to Ezra in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Give thanks</b> — His mercy endures (Ezra 3:11).', '<b>Thank Him for ruling over rulers</b> (Ezra 7:27).', '<b>Seek Him</b> — His hand is for good on those who do (Ezra 8:22).', '<b>Confess that He is righteous</b> (Ezra 9:15).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Ezra', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Ezra in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['The Return Under Cyrus (Chs. 1–2) — The decree. <i>(Ezra 1:1–3)</i>', 'The Altar and the House (Chs. 3–6) — Foundation and dedication. <i>(Ezra 3:2, 10–11; 6:16)</i>', 'Ezra Goes Up (Chs. 7–8) — The law prepared and taught. <i>(Ezra 7:6, 10, 27; 8:22)</i>', 'Confession and Separation (Chs. 9–10) — Righteous before Him. <i>(Ezra 9:15; 10:11)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Ezra, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward repentance, humility, and prayer without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Ezra simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, Ezra stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]

# Place in God's Storyline (Master Standard v1.155): last item of Section 1
from storyline import S as _STORY
